---
import LessonLayout from '../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
---

<LessonLayout
  title='Functional Kotlin'
  description='Lambdas, higher-order functions, and the collection ops (map, filter, reduce, groupBy) as pre-written HOFs.'
  number=5
  primary='#3d5a80'
  accent='#e07a5f'
>
  <div class="eyebrow">Kotlin · Functional style</div>
    <h1>Functional Kotlin, as <em>one</em> shift in thinking.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Lambdas, higher-order functions, <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>,
      <code>reduce</code>, <code>groupBy</code> — they look like six features, but
      they all rest on a single move: <strong>treat behavior as a value you can pass
      around, just like a number or a string.</strong>
    </p>

    <div class="meta">
      <span>⏱ ~11 min read</span>
      <span>🎯 Early intermediate</span>
      <span>🧠 Mental model first, syntax second</span>
    </div>

    <!-- THE BIG QUESTION -->
    <div class="question">
      <div class="question-label">The shift behind all six</div>
      <p class="question-text">
        What if a function could be a value — stored in a variable, passed as an
        argument, returned from another function?
        <br><br>
        Once that clicks, <em>every</em> feature on this page becomes "just a different
        way of using function-values."
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- THE BEHAVIOR-AS-VALUE DIAGRAM -->
    <div class="bav">
      <div class="bav-caption">The shift, in one picture</div>
      <div class="bav-row">
        <div class="bav-box data">
          <div class="lbl">Before</div>
          <div class="val">x = <span style="color: var(--sage);">42</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bav-arrow">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14"/><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="bav-box fn">
          <div class="lbl">After</div>
          <div class="val">f = <span style="color: var(--accent);">&#123; x → x + 1 &#125;</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bav-arrow">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14"/><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="bav-box both">
          <div class="lbl">Result</div>
          <div class="val" style="font-size: 13px;">A whole new category<br>of code</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="text-align: center; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 13.5px; margin: 16px 0 0;">
        A lambda is a function-value. A higher-order function is one that <em>accepts</em> or
        <em>returns</em> such a value. <code>map</code>/<code>filter</code>/<code>reduce</code>/<code>groupBy</code>
        are higher-order functions the standard library already wrote for you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 1. LAMBDAS -->
    <h2><span class="num">1</span>lambdas — behavior as a value</h2>

    <p>
      A lambda is just a function with no name. The braces say "here's a value."
      Inside, you declare the parameters with <code>-&gt;</code> and the body is
      the last expression (or whatever <code>return</code> statements you add).
    </p>

    <div class="concept" data-t="lambda">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 17s.5-4 2-4 1.5 4 3 4 2-4 4-4 1.5 4 3 4 2-4 4-4"/><path d="M4 17v3"/><path d="M20 17v3"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">lambda</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">&#123; params -> body &#125; · stored in variables, passed to functions, returned</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        A lambda is a value of type <code>(InputTypes) -&gt; ReturnType</code>.
        You can put it in a variable, hand it to a function, or build one on the fly.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// The four ways to write the same function-value</span>

  <span class="c">// 1. Stored in a variable</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">double</span>: (<span class="t">Int</span>) -&gt; <span class="t">Int</span> = &#123; <span class="v">x</span> -&gt; x * <span class="n">2</span> &#125;
  <span class="fn">double</span>(<span class="n">5</span>)   <span class="c">// 10</span>

  <span class="c">// 2. Single param shortcut: `it`</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">triple</span>: (<span class="t">Int</span>) -&gt; <span class="t">Int</span> = &#123; <span class="it">it</span> * <span class="n">3</span> &#125;

  <span class="c">// 3. Multi-param, explicit types</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">add</span>: (<span class="t">Int</span>, <span class="t">Int</span>) -&gt; <span class="t">Int</span> = &#123; a, b -&gt; a + b &#125;

  <span class="c">// 4. Inline, passed straight to a function (trailing-lambda syntax)</span>
  <span class="fn">listOf</span>(<span class="n">1</span>, <span class="n">2</span>, <span class="n">3</span>).<span class="fn">map</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span> * <span class="n">2</span> &#125;   <span class="c">// [2, 4, 6]</span></div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>The key mental model:</strong> <code>&#123; x -&gt; x + 1 &#125;</code> is a value
        the same way <code>42</code> is a value. You can hold it, hand it off, return it.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Trailing-lambda syntax</strong> is the reason Kotlin's collection ops
      read so well: when a function's <em>last parameter</em> is a function, you can
      move the lambda <em>outside</em> the parentheses. <code>map(items) &#123; it * 2 &#125;</code>
      is the same call as <code>map(items, &#123; it * 2 &#125;)</code>, but reads like a sentence.
    </div>

    <!-- 2. HIGHER-ORDER FUNCTIONS -->
    <h2><span class="num">2</span>higher-order functions — code that takes code</h2>

    <p>
      A higher-order function is a function whose <em>parameters</em> or
      <em>return type</em> is a function. The pattern is everywhere once you
      see it: <em>"I know what to loop over; you tell me what to do at each step."</em>
    </p>

    <div class="concept" data-t="hof">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M9 3v18"/><path d="M14 9h4"/><path d="M14 13h4"/><path d="M14 17h4"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">higher-order function (HOF)</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">fun ... (transform: (T) -> R): R — accepts or returns a function</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        You write the scaffolding. The caller fills in the behavior. This is the
        move that lets the standard library ship <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>,
        and the rest once, and you reuse them for any type and any transformation.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// Build your own "map" — it takes a function and a list, returns a list</span>
  <span class="k">fun</span> &lt;<span class="t">T</span>, <span class="t">R</span>&gt; <span class="t">List</span>&lt;<span class="t">T</span>&gt;.<span class="fn">myMap</span>(transform: (<span class="t">T</span>) -&gt; <span class="t">R</span>): <span class="t">List</span>&lt;<span class="t">R</span>&gt; &#123;
      <span class="k">val</span> out = <span class="t">ArrayList</span>&lt;<span class="t">R</span>&gt;(<span class="th">size</span>)
      <span class="k">for</span> (x <span class="k">in</span> <span class="th">this</span>) out.<span class="fn">add</span>(<span class="fn">transform</span>(x))
      <span class="k">return</span> out
  &#125;

  <span class="c">// Caller supplies the behavior:</span>
  <span class="fn">listOf</span>(<span class="n">1</span>, <span class="n">2</span>, <span class="n">3</span>).<span class="fn">myMap</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span> * <span class="n">10</span> &#125;   <span class="c">// [10, 20, 30]</span>
  <span class="fn">listOf</span>(<span class="n">1</span>, <span class="n">2</span>, <span class="n">3</span>).<span class="fn">myMap</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.<span class="fn">toString</span>() &#125;   <span class="c">// ["1", "2", "3"]</span></div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>The win:</strong> you wrote the loop <em>once</em>. The caller writes
        only the part that's unique to their case. This is why a tiny HOF + 100
        lambdas beats 100 hand-written loops.
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      The collection operations in the next section are exactly this — HOFs the
      standard library already wrote, specialized for the most common patterns.
      <code>map</code> = "loop and transform." <code>filter</code> = "loop and keep some."
      Once you see the pattern, you can read any of them.
    </p>

    <!-- 3-6. COLLECTION OPS - THE SHAPES -->
    <h2><span class="num">3</span>collection ops — HOFs the standard library wrote for you</h2>

    <p>
      Forget the names for a moment. Each of these is a HOF that walks a collection
      and applies your lambda. The difference is <strong>what shape comes out</strong>:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Same size, different type</strong> → <code>map</code></li>
      <li><strong>Smaller (or same), same type</strong> → <code>filter</code></li>
      <li><strong>One value, any type</strong> → <code>reduce</code> / <code>fold</code></li>
      <li><strong>Grouped into buckets</strong> → <code>groupBy</code></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="shapes">
      <div class="shapes-title">The four shapes — what comes out</div>
      <div class="shape-grid">

        <div class="shape-card">
          <div class="shape-name">map &#123; f &#125;</div>
          <div class="shape-shape">List&lt;T&gt; → List&lt;R&gt; · 1-to-1</div>
          <div class="shape-viz">
            <div class="dot">1</div>
            <div class="dot">2</div>
            <div class="dot">3</div>
            <span class="arrow">→</span>
            <div class="dot" style="background: var(--primary);">a</div>
            <div class="dot" style="background: var(--primary);">b</div>
            <div class="dot" style="background: var(--primary);">c</div>
          </div>
          <div class="shape-desc">
            Every element becomes <em>exactly one</em> new element. <code>map &#123; it * 2 &#125;</code>
            on <code>[1,2,3]</code> gives <code>[2,4,6]</code>. Same length, possibly different type.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="shape-card">
          <div class="shape-name">filter &#123; p &#125;</div>
          <div class="shape-shape">List&lt;T&gt; → List&lt;T&gt; · 1-to-0-or-1</div>
          <div class="shape-viz">
            <div class="dot">1</div>
            <div class="dot out">2</div>
            <div class="dot">3</div>
            <div class="dot out">4</div>
            <div class="dot">5</div>
            <span class="arrow">→</span>
            <div class="dot" style="background: #6a8aaf;">1</div>
            <div class="dot" style="background: #6a8aaf;">3</div>
            <div class="dot" style="background: #6a8aaf;">5</div>
          </div>
          <div class="shape-desc">
            Each element either <em>stays or goes</em> based on a predicate (lambda returning <code>Boolean</code>).
            <code>filter &#123; it % 2 == 1 &#125;</code> on odds.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="shape-card">
          <div class="shape-name">reduce &#123; acc, x -&gt; ... &#125;</div>
          <div class="shape-shape">List&lt;T&gt; → T · many-to-1</div>
          <div class="shape-viz">
            <div class="dot">1</div>
            <div class="dot">2</div>
            <div class="dot">3</div>
            <div class="dot">4</div>
            <span class="arrow">→</span>
            <div class="one">10</div>
          </div>
          <div class="shape-desc">
            Combine <em>all</em> elements into one. Starts with the first element as
            the accumulator. Same type in and out. <code>reduce &#123; a, b -&gt; a + b &#125;</code> on <code>[1,2,3,4]</code> gives <code>10</code>.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="shape-card">
          <div class="shape-name">groupBy &#123; k &#125;</div>
          <div class="shape-shape">List&lt;T&gt; → Map&lt;K, List&lt;T&gt;&gt; · 1-to-1 (but bucketed)</div>
          <div class="shape-viz">
            <div class="dot">a1</div>
            <div class="dot">b1</div>
            <div class="dot">a2</div>
            <div class="dot">b2</div>
            <span class="arrow">→</span>
            <div class="bucket">"a" → [a1, a2]</div>
            <div class="bucket">"b" → [b1, b2]</div>
          </div>
          <div class="shape-desc">
            Each element goes into <em>exactly one bucket</em>, keyed by what the lambda returns.
            The result is a <code>Map</code>. <code>groupBy &#123; it.first() &#125;</code> on words → words grouped by first letter.
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Read those four cards as <strong>one table</strong>: same input shape (a list),
      four different "what to do per element" lambdas, four different output shapes.
      The lambda is the variable. The op is the loop. The shape is the result.
    </p>

    <!-- Each in depth -->
    <h2><span class="num">M</span>map — the workhorse transform</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="map">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 6h18"/><path d="m9 6 3 14 3-14"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">map(transform)</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">List&lt;T&gt;.map &#123; T -&gt; R &#125; → List&lt;R&gt; · 1-to-1, each element transformed</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pair">
        <div>
          <div class="pair-label bad">Imperative</div>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> names = <span class="t">ArrayList</span>&lt;<span class="t">String</span>&gt;()
  <span class="k">for</span> (u <span class="k">in</span> users) &#123;
      names.<span class="fn">add</span>(u.name)
  &#125;</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="pair-label good">Functional</div>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> names = users.<span class="fn">map</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.name &#125;</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Same answer, one line. The version on the right says <em>what</em> you're
        doing, not <em>how</em> to do it. That's the whole point of functional style:
        describe the shape of the result, and let the library handle the iteration.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2><span class="num">F</span>filter — keep what passes the test</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="filter">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polygon points="22 3 2 3 10 12.46 10 19 14 21 14 12.46 22 3"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">filter(predicate)</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">List&lt;T&gt;.filter &#123; T -&gt; Boolean &#125; → List&lt;T&gt; · keep where true</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> active = users.<span class="fn">filter</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.active &#125;                    <span class="c">// keep if true</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> longNames = users.<span class="fn">filter</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.name.length &gt; <span class="n">5</span> &#125;      <span class="c">// any condition</span>

  <span class="c">// filterNot, filterIndexed, filterIsInstance&lt;X&gt;() are variations on the same idea.</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> failures = results.<span class="fn">filterIsInstance</span>&lt;<span class="t">Result</span>.<span class="t">Failure</span>&gt;()</div>

      <p>
        The lambda must return <code>Boolean</code> — that's the test. Same input type,
        smaller output. Combine with <code>map</code> to filter-and-transform in one go.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2><span class="num">R</span>reduce &amp; fold — squash to one value</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="reduce">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M12 3v18"/><path d="M3 12h18"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">reduce &#123; acc, x -&gt; next &#125; &nbsp;/&nbsp; fold(initial) &#123; acc, x -&gt; next &#125;</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">List&lt;T&gt; → T · all elements combined into one</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// reduce: starts with the first element. Same type in and out.</span>
  <span class="fn">listOf</span>(<span class="n">1</span>, <span class="n">2</span>, <span class="n">3</span>, <span class="n">4</span>).<span class="fn">reduce</span> &#123; acc, x -&gt; acc + x &#125;   <span class="c">// 10</span>

  <span class="c">// fold: you supply the starting value, and the result type can differ.</span>
  <span class="fn">listOf</span>(<span class="n">1</span>, <span class="n">2</span>, <span class="n">3</span>).<span class="fn">fold</span>(<span class="n">0</span>) &#123; acc, x -&gt; acc + x &#125;   <span class="c">// 6, starting from 0</span>
  <span class="fn">listOf</span>(<span class="s">"a"</span>, <span class="s">"b"</span>, <span class="s">"c"</span>).<span class="fn">fold</span>(<span class="s">"start: "</span>) &#123; acc, x -&gt; <span class="s">"</span><span class="th">$acc</span><span class="s">,</span><span class="th">$x</span><span class="s">"</span> &#125;
  <span class="c">// → "start: ,a,b,c"</span></div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>When to pick which:</strong> <code>reduce</code> for "combine same-type things
        using the first one as the seed" (sum, max, concat). <code>fold</code> for "I have
        an initial value" or "the result type differs from the input type" (build a
        string, build a map, count into a different accumulator).
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2><span class="num">G</span>groupBy — partition by a key</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="groupBy">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="3" width="7" height="7"/><rect x="14" y="3" width="7" height="7"/><rect x="14" y="14" width="7" height="7"/><rect x="3" y="14" width="7" height="7"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">groupBy(keyFn)</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">List&lt;T&gt; → Map&lt;K, List&lt;T&gt;&gt; · one bucket per distinct key</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> byDept = employees.<span class="fn">groupBy</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.department &#125;
  <span class="c">// Map&lt;String, List&lt;Employee&gt;&gt;  — one list per department</span>

  <span class="k">val</span> byLength = words.<span class="fn">groupBy</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.length &#125;
  <span class="c">// Map&lt;Int, List&lt;String&gt;&gt; — words of length 3, length 4, etc.</span>

  <span class="c">// groupBy can also produce a different value type per bucket:</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> nameByDept = employees.<span class="fn">groupBy</span>(&#123; <span class="it">it</span>.department &#125;, &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.name &#125;)
  <span class="c">// Map&lt;String, List&lt;String&gt;&gt;</span></div>

      <p>
        The lambda returns the <em>key</em> the element belongs in. Same number of
        elements out, but now structured as a lookup. Often followed by
        <code>mapValues</code> to compute a per-bucket summary.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// "How many people per department?" — groupBy + mapValues + size</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> countByDept = employees
      .<span class="fn">groupBy</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.department &#125;
      .<span class="fn">mapValues</span> &#123; (_, list) -&gt; list.size &#125;
  <span class="c">// Map&lt;String, Int&gt;</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- PIPELINE -->
    <h2>Composing: the pipeline</h2>
    <p>
      None of this is interesting in isolation. The win is that every op <em>returns
      a collection (or value)</em> you can pipe into the next op. Read the chain
      left-to-right as a story about your data.
    </p>

    <div class="pipeline">
      <div class="pipe-step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-name">.filter &#123; it.active &#125;</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Keep only the active users. Smaller list, same type.</div>
          <div class="step-returns">Returns: <code>List&lt;User&gt;</code></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipe-step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-name">.map &#123; it.name &#125;</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Pull the name out. Same length, different type.</div>
          <div class="step-returns">Returns: <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipe-step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-name">.sorted()</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Alphabetize. Same length, same type, reordered.</div>
          <div class="step-returns">Returns: <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipe-step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-name">.take(5)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Keep the first five. Shorter list.</div>
          <div class="step-returns">Returns: <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="code" style="margin-top: 20px;"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> topFive = users
      .<span class="fn">filter</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.active &#125;
      .<span class="fn">map</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.name &#125;
      .<span class="fn">sorted</span>()
      .<span class="fn">take</span>(<span class="n">5</span>)</div>

      <p style="text-align: center; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 13.5px; margin: 16px 0 0;">
        Each step is a verb. Read it like a sentence: "filter to active users,
        map to their names, sort, take the top five." No <code>for</code> loop in sight.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- THE SHIFT DIAGRAM -->
    <h2>The mental shift, side-by-side</h2>
    <p>
      Functional style isn't "shorter imperative code." It's a different question.
      Imperative asks <em>"what steps do I take?"</em> Functional asks
      <em>"what shape should the result be?"</em>
    </p>

    <div class="shift">
      <div class="shift-row">
        <div class="shift-col imp">
          <h4>Imperative</h4>
          <p>You write the loop. You mutate a variable. The reader has to simulate the steps.</p>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> total = <span class="n">0</span>
  <span class="k">for</span> (n <span class="k">in</span> numbers) &#123;
      total += n
  &#125;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="shift-col func">
          <h4>Functional</h4>
          <p>You describe the result. The library walks the data. The reader sees the shape.</p>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> total = numbers.<span class="fn">sum</span>()     <span class="c">// or</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> total = numbers.<span class="fn">reduce</span> &#123; a, b -&gt; a + b &#125;</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>When the functional style shines:</strong> when you're transforming,
      filtering, summarizing, or grouping data. <strong>When to drop back to imperative:</strong>
      when you need early-exit, side effects mid-loop, or a complex local state machine
      that's hard to express as a chain. Both tools, same language.
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION TREE -->
    <h2>Picking the right op: a 30-second guide</h2>

    <div class="tree">
      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q1 · One element out?</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">"Sum it / find the max / build a string / count."</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ reduce (same type) / fold (different type or with seed)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q2 · Group elements by some category?</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">"Group users by department. Bucket words by first letter."</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ groupBy &#123; keyFn &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q3 · Same number of elements, but each one is different?</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">"Pull the name out. Double every number. Convert to JSON."</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ map &#123; transform &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q4 · Smaller list — keep only the ones that pass a test?</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">"Only the active users. Only words longer than 5 chars."</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ filter &#123; predicate &#125;</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pair">
      <div>
        <div class="pair-label good">Often combined</div>
        <div class="code">items
      .<span class="fn">filter</span> &#123; ... &#125;
      .<span class="fn">map</span> &#123; ... &#125;
      .<span class="fn">groupBy</span> &#123; ... &#125;</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="pair-label neutral">For sequences: asSequence()</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="c">// Each op on a List builds a new list.</span>
  <span class="c">// asSequence() fuses them — lazy, one pass, big perf win on huge data.</span>
  items.<span class="fn">asSequence</span>()
      .<span class="fn">filter</span> &#123; ... &#125;
      .<span class="fn">map</span> &#123; ... &#125;
      .<span class="fn">toList</span>()</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- RECIPES -->
    <h2>Common patterns (copy these)</h2>

    <div class="recipes">
      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Sum / count with reduce</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Collapse a list to one value.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> total = prices.<span class="fn">reduce</span> &#123; a, b -&gt; a + b &#125;
  <span class="c">// or just: prices.sum()</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Filter + map pipeline</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Keep the ones you want, project to the field you need.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> names = users
      .<span class="fn">filter</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.age &gt;= <span class="n">18</span> &#125;
      .<span class="fn">map</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.name &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Index lookup with associateBy</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Turn a list into a map keyed by some field.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> byId = users.<span class="fn">associateBy</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.id &#125;
  <span class="c">// Map&lt;Long, User&gt;  — byId[42L] in O(1)</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Per-group summary</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">groupBy then mapValues to compute something per bucket.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> avgAgeByDept = employees
      .<span class="fn">groupBy</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.dept &#125;
      .<span class="fn">mapValues</span> &#123; (_, list) -&gt; list.<span class="fn">map</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.age &#125;.<span class="fn">average</span>() &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">firstOrNull with a predicate</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Find one matching element, or null.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> admin = users.<span class="fn">firstOrNull</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span>.role == <span class="s">"admin"</span> &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">partition: two lists in one pass</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Split a list into "passed" and "failed" by a test.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> (passed, failed) = scores.<span class="fn">partition</span> &#123; <span class="it">it</span> &gt;= <span class="n">60</span> &#125;</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- RECAP -->
    <div class="recap">
      <h2><span class="num">↺</span>Recap in one minute</h2>
      <p>
        Six features, one shift: <em>treat behavior as a value.</em> Everything on this
        page is a different angle on that single move.
      </p>
      <div class="recap-grid">
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">lambda</div>
          <div class="desc">A function-as-value, written in <code>&#123; x -&gt; ... &#125;</code>. You can store it, pass it, return it. Trailing-lambda syntax makes the call sites read like English.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">higher-order function</div>
          <div class="desc">A function that takes or returns a function. Separates <em>what to do at each step</em> (the caller's lambda) from <em>how to walk the data</em> (the HOF's job).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">map</div>
          <div class="desc">1-to-1. Every element becomes one new element. Same length, possibly different type.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">filter</div>
          <div class="desc">1-to-0-or-1. Keep elements where the lambda returns <code>true</code>. Smaller (or same) list.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">reduce / fold</div>
          <div class="desc">Many-to-1. Combine all elements into one. <code>reduce</code> seeds with the first element; <code>fold</code> takes an explicit seed and can change type.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">groupBy</div>
          <div class="desc">1-to-1 bucketed. Each element lands in exactly one bucket. Returns a <code>Map&lt;K, List&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> — often followed by <code>mapValues</code> for per-bucket summaries.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="end">
      One shift, many tools: <em>behavior becomes a value, the library walks the data,
      and you describe the shape of the result instead of writing the loop.</em>
    </div>
</LessonLayout>

